WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:08.580 --> 00:00:10.710
- So I'm gonna say just a few words

00:00:10.710 --> 00:00:13.730
about where I'm coming from,

00:00:13.730 --> 00:00:15.330
and then I'll give ya just a few notes

00:00:15.330 --> 00:00:17.270
on what I've learned along the way.

00:00:17.270 --> 00:00:19.210
And then I'll talk a bit
about what I hope to do

00:00:19.210 --> 00:00:21.483
for communications here at Yale.

00:00:24.490 --> 00:00:26.300
My career began in 1992.

00:00:26.300 --> 00:00:29.410
This is three years before
the IPO of Netscape.

00:00:29.410 --> 00:00:31.660
So I was, when I kind of look back on it,

00:00:31.660 --> 00:00:34.122
I was actually one of a generation

00:00:34.122 --> 00:00:37.290
that knew what it was like
to work in non-digital world

00:00:37.290 --> 00:00:39.390
before it knew a digital world.

00:00:39.390 --> 00:00:42.350
So 1992 was a famously bad job market.

00:00:42.350 --> 00:00:44.750
I wanted to be an editor,
I wanted to be a writer,

00:00:44.750 --> 00:00:46.200
I wanted to get in any way I could,

00:00:46.200 --> 00:00:48.870
so I got in through the
backdoor which was production.

00:00:48.870 --> 00:00:50.540
And so this is a typesetter.

00:00:50.540 --> 00:00:53.673
This is, I'm not sure
what vintage this is.

00:00:54.580 --> 00:00:57.030
What I did for a living for a year was I,

00:00:57.030 --> 00:00:57.940
I wish I had it with me.

00:00:57.940 --> 00:01:00.640
I had this thing called
a pica ruler, okay.

00:01:00.640 --> 00:01:02.960
And I was given layouts from designers

00:01:02.960 --> 00:01:04.360
who were using QuarkXPress,

00:01:04.360 --> 00:01:06.371
which was this brand new layout program.

00:01:06.371 --> 00:01:07.204
(audience laughing)

00:01:07.204 --> 00:01:08.260
You guys remember this, right?

00:01:09.978 --> 00:01:13.610
But the digital path
had only gone that far.

00:01:13.610 --> 00:01:17.025
We were still producing
magazines with photostack cameras

00:01:17.025 --> 00:01:19.550
and pulling repro and filming it.

00:01:19.550 --> 00:01:22.160
And so somebody had to
take this new layout

00:01:22.160 --> 00:01:23.400
from QuarkXPress,

00:01:23.400 --> 00:01:26.160
measure it with a pica
ruler and write code

00:01:26.160 --> 00:01:31.160
to spit out the right film in a processor.

00:01:31.180 --> 00:01:33.460
I've seen a couple of people
nodding with fondness,

00:01:33.460 --> 00:01:34.391
so I've got a pop quiz.

00:01:34.391 --> 00:01:35.224
(audience laughing)

00:01:35.224 --> 00:01:36.057
Which is great, which is great.

00:01:36.057 --> 00:01:38.660
So how many, for those who
are nodding in fondness,

00:01:38.660 --> 00:01:40.913
I ask you how many points are in a pica?

00:01:42.165 --> 00:01:43.210
- [Man] 72.

00:01:43.210 --> 00:01:44.122
- Huh?

00:01:44.122 --> 00:01:44.955
- [Man] 72.

00:01:44.955 --> 00:01:46.210
- That's how many points are in an inch,

00:01:46.210 --> 00:01:47.043
but that's really good.

00:01:47.043 --> 00:01:48.300
(audience laughing)

00:01:48.300 --> 00:01:51.310
- 12 points in a pica,
six picas to an inch.

00:01:51.310 --> 00:01:52.320
Yeah, you guys remember this?

00:01:52.320 --> 00:01:53.878
Okay, so anyway, that's how I started.

00:01:53.878 --> 00:01:55.800
And one thing led to another

00:01:55.800 --> 00:01:59.390
and I did actually make it
on to the editorial side.

00:01:59.390 --> 00:02:01.290
This was a few years later.

00:02:01.290 --> 00:02:03.948
So I had done some editorial work,

00:02:03.948 --> 00:02:05.781
editorial production work

00:02:05.781 --> 00:02:08.530
for the New York Times
Women's Magazine Group,

00:02:08.530 --> 00:02:10.170
met the woman who would become my wife,

00:02:10.170 --> 00:02:11.040
I moved up to Boston,

00:02:11.040 --> 00:02:13.730
I thought this was the end
of my publishing career,

00:02:13.730 --> 00:02:14.616
and I really did.

00:02:14.616 --> 00:02:16.960
And lo and behold, I got a call

00:02:16.960 --> 00:02:18.380
to become the managing editor

00:02:18.380 --> 00:02:22.010
of this really cool business
magazine called Fast Company.

00:02:22.010 --> 00:02:23.710
If you ask the founders
what this thing was,

00:02:23.710 --> 00:02:26.490
they said it was what they imagined to be,

00:02:26.490 --> 00:02:31.490
or the results that was Fast
Company came from them saying

00:02:32.040 --> 00:02:35.935
what if Businessweek and
Rolling Stone had a lovechild?

00:02:35.935 --> 00:02:36.986
(audience laughing)

00:02:36.986 --> 00:02:38.853
- All right, that this was that.

00:02:42.370 --> 00:02:45.360
But the serious thing
about Fast Company was,

00:02:45.360 --> 00:02:48.920
so again, it was started just
before the internet took off,

00:02:48.920 --> 00:02:50.740
but it was kind of bottling this energy

00:02:50.740 --> 00:02:53.590
that technology was about to unleash.

00:02:53.590 --> 00:02:55.900
And it was powerful stuff.

00:02:55.900 --> 00:02:57.420
I mean, I think a lot of the folks

00:02:57.420 --> 00:02:58.560
in this room remember that.

00:02:58.560 --> 00:03:02.400
The sense of possibility that
technology was unleashing was,

00:03:02.400 --> 00:03:03.720
it seemed unbounded,

00:03:03.720 --> 00:03:06.420
and of course the markets
responded in an unbounded way

00:03:06.420 --> 00:03:08.640
that then was bounded, right?

00:03:08.640 --> 00:03:09.820
But still it was really exciting,

00:03:09.820 --> 00:03:13.303
and I guess the note I wanna make here is

00:03:13.303 --> 00:03:16.210
this was a very important cultural moment.

00:03:16.210 --> 00:03:18.240
And so I remember when
I was at Fast Company,

00:03:18.240 --> 00:03:20.160
we had a poster and we had

00:03:20.160 --> 00:03:22.810
like, obviously amazing designers,

00:03:22.810 --> 00:03:25.570
and the poster had this
really cool depiction

00:03:25.570 --> 00:03:27.650
of a quote from Hunter S. Thompson,

00:03:27.650 --> 00:03:29.960
the founder of gonzo journalism, right.

00:03:29.960 --> 00:03:31.123
And the quote was,

00:03:32.707 --> 00:03:34.127
"faster, faster,

00:03:34.127 --> 00:03:38.173
"until the thrill of speed
overcomes the fear of death."

00:03:38.173 --> 00:03:39.006
- [Woman] Whoa.

00:03:39.006 --> 00:03:40.530
- Right, yeah exactly!

00:03:40.530 --> 00:03:41.710
Like that's what it felt like.

00:03:41.710 --> 00:03:43.640
So that was pretty amazing.

00:03:43.640 --> 00:03:48.210
Okay, and then after
Fast Company was bought

00:03:48.210 --> 00:03:51.380
for 375 million dollars in
the teeth of the recession,

00:03:51.380 --> 00:03:56.380
one of the most ill-timed
purchases in magazine history,

00:03:56.827 --> 00:04:00.180
you know, unlucky for
the buyer unfortunately.

00:04:00.180 --> 00:04:02.553
But the magazine still
lives on, which is awesome.

00:04:04.659 --> 00:04:08.050
I then decided not to move to
New York with Fast Company.

00:04:08.050 --> 00:04:10.573
I'm again in Boston,
thinking what am I gonna do?

00:04:11.960 --> 00:04:13.280
It's not long before I get a call

00:04:13.280 --> 00:04:17.220
from the newly-arrived
editor-in-chief of Technology Review.

00:04:17.220 --> 00:04:19.700
And he and I had a natural
reason to like each other

00:04:19.700 --> 00:04:22.200
because he has been editor-in-chief

00:04:22.200 --> 00:04:24.388
of a magazine called Red Herring

00:04:24.388 --> 00:04:26.830
back when I was managing
editor of Fast Company.

00:04:26.830 --> 00:04:28.560
Red Herring was the kind of bible

00:04:28.560 --> 00:04:31.520
of the kind of frothy deal-making culture

00:04:31.520 --> 00:04:33.370
of Silicon Valley in the 90s

00:04:33.370 --> 00:04:37.678
when IPOs were a many-day,

00:04:37.678 --> 00:04:40.263
many-times-a-day kind of an affair.

00:04:42.328 --> 00:04:46.640
So I was asked to help
reimagine this magazine,

00:04:46.640 --> 00:04:48.990
which it was a storied magazine for MIT,

00:04:48.990 --> 00:04:52.180
had always been an independently-run,

00:04:52.180 --> 00:04:53.830
interesting tech magazine.

00:04:53.830 --> 00:04:54.760
But the point I want to make here

00:04:54.760 --> 00:04:57.130
is this was sort of after the crash.

00:04:57.130 --> 00:05:01.900
Okay, this was, when I'm
called for this it's 2004.

00:05:01.900 --> 00:05:03.230
So we've been through the crash.

00:05:03.230 --> 00:05:07.740
We know that technology is
not going to change markets.

00:05:07.740 --> 00:05:09.170
That there still will be crashes.

00:05:09.170 --> 00:05:10.170
There still will be

00:05:12.854 --> 00:05:15.143
the normal characteristics of an economy.

00:05:17.520 --> 00:05:21.680
But it was sort of a master
class in technology for me,

00:05:21.680 --> 00:05:23.060
which was great.

00:05:23.060 --> 00:05:26.800
Okay, this is where my career
takes a right-hand turn,

00:05:26.800 --> 00:05:31.770
and the person who would
take my career and turn it

00:05:31.770 --> 00:05:33.420
was a woman named Susan Hockfield.

00:05:34.850 --> 00:05:38.073
Susan, you might know, had
a long career here at Yale,

00:05:38.920 --> 00:05:43.920
was an exceptional scientist,
was Provost of this university

00:05:44.050 --> 00:05:49.050
and was recruited to serve as
MIT's 16th President in 2004.

00:05:50.470 --> 00:05:53.330
She is one of the most
important people in my life

00:05:53.330 --> 00:05:54.610
because she changed it

00:05:54.610 --> 00:05:56.384
and because she is one
of the finest leaders

00:05:56.384 --> 00:05:58.360
I will ever work for.

00:05:58.360 --> 00:06:00.580
So, how does that look?

00:06:00.580 --> 00:06:05.070
Okay, so this image is like,
this doesn't really depict MIT.

00:06:05.070 --> 00:06:08.370
This is not what I think of
exactly when I think of MIT.

00:06:08.370 --> 00:06:10.780
It is the iconic image of the Great Dome,

00:06:10.780 --> 00:06:15.780
but MIT is gritty and it
is all tech all the time.

00:06:16.280 --> 00:06:18.780
And my arrival there taught me

00:06:18.780 --> 00:06:21.113
what it meant to be at
a research university.

00:06:22.980 --> 00:06:26.850
And so you might say, as I did,

00:06:26.850 --> 00:06:30.010
what on earth, what do you know, right?

00:06:30.010 --> 00:06:32.115
Why would it make sense for
you to do communications

00:06:32.115 --> 00:06:34.680
at this great university?

00:06:34.680 --> 00:06:37.040
You're not a PR person, you're an editor.

00:06:37.040 --> 00:06:41.530
And you don't know anything
about universities.

00:06:41.530 --> 00:06:43.940
So those are two strikes.

00:06:43.940 --> 00:06:45.369
And I was equally baffled.

00:06:45.369 --> 00:06:47.228
I thought, that's a good question, right?

00:06:47.228 --> 00:06:50.140
(audience laughing)

00:06:50.140 --> 00:06:51.087
But here's what I had.

00:06:51.087 --> 00:06:55.050
I had a simple contention
that was based on technology,

00:06:55.050 --> 00:06:57.800
which is why I raise it here,
based on digital technology.

00:06:57.800 --> 00:07:00.720
Central contention I had was that,

00:07:00.720 --> 00:07:02.640
and by the way this is not, this is like,

00:07:02.640 --> 00:07:04.307
you guys are all gonna
be like, yes, of course.

00:07:04.307 --> 00:07:06.783
But the contention I had was,

00:07:08.220 --> 00:07:11.110
digital technology had unleashed a power

00:07:11.110 --> 00:07:14.948
for people to communicate that was,

00:07:14.948 --> 00:07:17.510
and I don't use this word
lightly, it was revolutionary.

00:07:17.510 --> 00:07:20.370
And this was as true for organizations

00:07:20.370 --> 00:07:22.010
as it was for individuals, right?

00:07:22.010 --> 00:07:23.870
We often think of the
power that the internet

00:07:23.870 --> 00:07:26.570
has given individuals to have a voice,

00:07:26.570 --> 00:07:29.270
which of course is true.

00:07:29.270 --> 00:07:31.670
But it also gave organizations that power.

00:07:31.670 --> 00:07:35.330
So all of a sudden, a
place like MIT, I felt,

00:07:35.330 --> 00:07:37.173
could tell its own story.

00:07:38.270 --> 00:07:40.270
And a kind of a part B

00:07:40.270 --> 00:07:44.940
to that change of the
world of communications was

00:07:44.940 --> 00:07:47.650
and this was kind of a hunch on my part,

00:07:47.650 --> 00:07:50.800
and it turned out to be correct
at least in the case of MIT,

00:07:50.800 --> 00:07:54.870
the part B was, reading
habits were changing.

00:07:54.870 --> 00:07:57.330
People would read your content

00:07:58.230 --> 00:08:00.980
even if you were demonstrably biased,

00:08:00.980 --> 00:08:02.433
if that content was amazing.

00:08:04.940 --> 00:08:06.440
So I said, well, I don't know anything

00:08:06.440 --> 00:08:09.050
about what we're doing
here as a university,

00:08:09.050 --> 00:08:10.090
but what I do know is this.

00:08:10.090 --> 00:08:13.310
I might be able to build
a story-telling machine

00:08:14.170 --> 00:08:16.130
that this place has not seen before.

00:08:16.130 --> 00:08:18.800
And I will hire reporters
who are every bit as good

00:08:18.800 --> 00:08:20.720
as the reporters in the New York Times,

00:08:20.720 --> 00:08:22.420
and they will write
under a beat structure,

00:08:22.420 --> 00:08:23.890
and they will get to know their areas

00:08:23.890 --> 00:08:25.300
better than anyone at the New York Times

00:08:25.300 --> 00:08:27.010
so that when we send them a press release

00:08:27.010 --> 00:08:28.450
on computer science,

00:08:28.450 --> 00:08:29.930
nobody at the New York Times is thinking,

00:08:29.930 --> 00:08:32.010
look at these lightweight PR flacks

00:08:32.010 --> 00:08:34.750
sending me stuff about computer science.

00:08:34.750 --> 00:08:37.450
What they instead say is,
oh good, something from MIT.

00:08:39.300 --> 00:08:41.510
And the writers I hired are still there

00:08:41.510 --> 00:08:43.630
because why are they still there?

00:08:43.630 --> 00:08:45.480
Because they get to write
about what they love,

00:08:45.480 --> 00:08:48.780
and there aren't, you know,
buyouts and layoffs and all that

00:08:48.780 --> 00:08:50.720
that they experience in
the publishing industry,

00:08:50.720 --> 00:08:51.853
so it works, okay.

00:08:53.552 --> 00:08:56.460
But anyway, my charge
from Susan, I like to say,

00:08:56.460 --> 00:08:58.500
I've said this back to her
in a kind of a joking way,

00:08:58.500 --> 00:09:00.240
like I'm exaggerating, surely.

00:09:00.240 --> 00:09:01.380
And she looks back at me like,

00:09:01.380 --> 00:09:03.030
yeah, that's what I told you to do, right.

00:09:03.030 --> 00:09:06.120
So what she told me was,

00:09:06.120 --> 00:09:08.969
make everything better
and cut the budget 15%.

00:09:08.969 --> 00:09:11.010
(audience laughing)

00:09:11.010 --> 00:09:14.780
And this 15% was across MIT.

00:09:14.780 --> 00:09:16.880
This was after the crash of 2010,

00:09:16.880 --> 00:09:20.990
and so the endowment
had suffered a 17% hit

00:09:20.990 --> 00:09:23.340
and so everybody was
taking it on the chin,

00:09:23.340 --> 00:09:26.120
and so we tried to do all of that.

00:09:26.120 --> 00:09:26.953
Okay.

00:09:33.010 --> 00:09:36.170
So then, I'm sort of doing
this for a while, right.

00:09:36.170 --> 00:09:38.190
So I kind of rose up through the ranks,

00:09:38.190 --> 00:09:39.700
which was great because I got to learn

00:09:39.700 --> 00:09:43.040
how you do what I do now slowly over time.

00:09:43.040 --> 00:09:44.900
I think I had four
positions in that office,

00:09:44.900 --> 00:09:46.070
ending as Vice President,

00:09:46.070 --> 00:09:47.353
essentially the first Vice President

00:09:47.353 --> 00:09:51.900
through communications for
MIT, and I simply loved it.

00:09:51.900 --> 00:09:52.883
Happy as a clam.

00:09:53.970 --> 00:09:58.970
And then I got a call from Yale,

00:09:59.799 --> 00:10:02.635
and I thought, well, that's
odd, I'm perfectly happy.

00:10:02.635 --> 00:10:05.563
I don't need to, I'm fine.

00:10:07.030 --> 00:10:11.960
But boy, when President Salovey
begins to explain to you

00:10:11.960 --> 00:10:14.140
his vision, that becomes a conversation

00:10:14.140 --> 00:10:15.540
that you do not want to end.

00:10:17.587 --> 00:10:19.873
He's a great President.

00:10:21.390 --> 00:10:25.180
He's got a great vision, and
he's got an amazing team.

00:10:25.180 --> 00:10:27.910
And I'd put everyone in
this room on that team.

00:10:27.910 --> 00:10:30.954
So boy it was hard to say goodbye

00:10:30.954 --> 00:10:34.380
to this place that I loved, MIT,

00:10:34.380 --> 00:10:37.520
but the more I spoke to
Peter and the team here,

00:10:37.520 --> 00:10:39.390
the more excited I was
about the possibilities.

00:10:39.390 --> 00:10:40.930
And the conversations were quite natural

00:10:40.930 --> 00:10:42.710
because it felt to me, and we'll see,

00:10:42.710 --> 00:10:44.110
these are early days, right?

00:10:44.110 --> 00:10:47.830
It felt to me like the things
that he was hoping most for

00:10:47.830 --> 00:10:51.331
were things that perhaps I
had some experience doing.

00:10:51.331 --> 00:10:54.750
So I didn't, I'm getting
to a point in my career

00:10:54.750 --> 00:10:56.300
where I feel less like I'm faking it.

00:10:56.300 --> 00:10:59.473
But it's still, it still sneaks in.

00:11:03.400 --> 00:11:06.111
And let me say this, I was compelled,

00:11:06.111 --> 00:11:09.180
quite personally compelled,
but I'll say this,

00:11:09.180 --> 00:11:10.840
and this is gonna sound a little
grandiose, but I don't care

00:11:10.840 --> 00:11:13.320
'cause I actually think it's true,

00:11:13.320 --> 00:11:14.873
I think our country needs Yale.

00:11:15.780 --> 00:11:16.613
Right?

00:11:16.613 --> 00:11:18.940
I think our country
really needs this place.

00:11:18.940 --> 00:11:20.801
The world does, too, for sure.

00:11:20.801 --> 00:11:23.520
I don't mean to compete with that notion.

00:11:23.520 --> 00:11:25.650
But our country needs our
great civic institutions,

00:11:25.650 --> 00:11:27.423
the ones who have produced leaders,

00:11:28.570 --> 00:11:30.560
and this is a very special
place for that reason.

00:11:30.560 --> 00:11:35.250
And so, for what it's worth,
that is in the back of my mind

00:11:35.250 --> 00:11:38.250
is that we, all of us have the power,

00:11:38.250 --> 00:11:42.551
to be of enormous service through what is

00:11:42.551 --> 00:11:47.551
a true national treasure
and also a global treasure.

00:11:48.820 --> 00:11:52.873
Not a lot right now is going
super well in the country.

00:11:55.110 --> 00:11:56.240
What remains unchanged

00:11:56.240 --> 00:11:58.340
is that our great research universities

00:11:58.340 --> 00:11:59.640
are the envy of the world.

00:12:00.580 --> 00:12:02.530
And that is powerful stuff.

00:12:02.530 --> 00:12:04.470
Okay, that wasn't too grandiose, was it?

00:12:04.470 --> 00:12:05.593
Is that okay?

00:12:05.593 --> 00:12:06.426
(audience laughing)

00:12:06.426 --> 00:12:07.826
All right, good, okay, good.

00:12:09.500 --> 00:12:12.193
And just, I'll sort of,

00:12:15.910 --> 00:12:18.334
I won't get into specifics,
but let me just say,

00:12:18.334 --> 00:12:20.240
the gist of the conversation

00:12:20.240 --> 00:12:23.710
I was having with President Salovey was,

00:12:23.710 --> 00:12:25.790
to the degree that we
can, we in communications

00:12:25.790 --> 00:12:28.490
will whiff to play on the
offensive side of the field.

00:12:29.400 --> 00:12:31.393
There are all kinds of reasons why,

00:12:31.393 --> 00:12:33.400
in communications at Yale,

00:12:33.400 --> 00:12:36.490
we kind of have to be a
little bit on the defensive.

00:12:36.490 --> 00:12:39.240
People are quite interested in this place,

00:12:39.240 --> 00:12:43.690
and we can become proxy for
some cultural disagreements.

00:12:43.690 --> 00:12:46.280
There are things to really like about that

00:12:46.280 --> 00:12:48.090
because it signals the
importance of this place.

00:12:48.090 --> 00:12:49.160
But to the degree that we can,

00:12:49.160 --> 00:12:51.650
we need to be out there telling our story

00:12:51.650 --> 00:12:53.690
and engaging with audiences, okay.

00:12:53.690 --> 00:12:56.023
More on that as we go.

00:12:59.160 --> 00:13:01.450
All right, so I'm just gonna quickly,

00:13:01.450 --> 00:13:03.930
my goal is tripartite, if you will.

00:13:03.930 --> 00:13:06.210
I'm just gonna read these out loud.

00:13:06.210 --> 00:13:08.581
This is what it is all about

00:13:08.581 --> 00:13:11.090
in terms of communications here.

00:13:11.090 --> 00:13:13.760
We need to inform and
inspire the Yale community.

00:13:13.760 --> 00:13:17.520
I think of our audiences as concentric

00:13:17.520 --> 00:13:20.960
and this is the center, full stop,

00:13:20.960 --> 00:13:22.793
of what we must be interested in.

00:13:24.670 --> 00:13:26.910
Having done that, we need
to attract the finest talent

00:13:26.910 --> 00:13:28.440
in the world to join it.

00:13:28.440 --> 00:13:30.750
All good things happen
at a place like this

00:13:30.750 --> 00:13:33.610
because we continue to pull
in the world's best talent.

00:13:33.610 --> 00:13:36.120
And that's true for students,
it's true for faculty,

00:13:36.120 --> 00:13:38.540
and it's true for staff, okay.

00:13:38.540 --> 00:13:40.433
It is all about that.

00:13:41.740 --> 00:13:42.940
Okay?

00:13:42.940 --> 00:13:45.080
And then, of course, we need
to burnish our reputation

00:13:45.080 --> 00:13:47.310
across the country and around the world.

00:13:47.310 --> 00:13:49.910
And this isn't just a oh,
things are nice in threes

00:13:49.910 --> 00:13:51.410
and so I better put a third thing on.

00:13:51.410 --> 00:13:53.960
I do see this as
distinguished from the second

00:13:53.960 --> 00:13:57.470
because in the second of
the three things I said,

00:13:57.470 --> 00:13:58.620
I said we need to bring people in

00:13:58.620 --> 00:14:00.910
who are gonna be part of this community.

00:14:00.910 --> 00:14:03.480
But there are other people
whose sentiment and behavior

00:14:03.480 --> 00:14:06.830
we need to move who are actually
not ever gonna come here

00:14:06.830 --> 00:14:09.310
and be part of this, but
who can vastly influence

00:14:09.310 --> 00:14:11.280
our fortunes for good or for ill.

00:14:11.280 --> 00:14:12.795
So for instance, a member
of Congress voting on a bill

00:14:12.795 --> 00:14:16.590
that matters to us like a
tax on the endowment, right?

00:14:16.590 --> 00:14:19.500
So we need to influence
people who won't come here

00:14:19.500 --> 00:14:21.420
but who can influence our fate.

00:14:21.420 --> 00:14:23.770
Okay so I'm just gonna make a couple of,

00:14:23.770 --> 00:14:25.730
give you a couple of notes

00:14:25.730 --> 00:14:28.060
about what I've learned along the way.

00:14:28.060 --> 00:14:32.624
And then I hope I'll have time for Q &amp; A.

00:14:32.624 --> 00:14:36.430
After the fact, it's very
important for the filming, okay?

00:14:36.430 --> 00:14:38.700
Okay, so people crave,

00:14:38.700 --> 00:14:40.100
these notes are, by the way,

00:14:40.100 --> 00:14:42.100
I did sort of think of them in terms of,

00:14:44.629 --> 00:14:48.200
as wonderfully distributed
as our audience is today,

00:14:48.200 --> 00:14:49.370
as John pointed out,

00:14:49.370 --> 00:14:51.420
we're all here kind of to
talk about digital things,

00:14:51.420 --> 00:14:53.310
and so I tried to pull out

00:14:55.310 --> 00:14:58.300
things that I think bear on
that question of being digital.

00:14:58.300 --> 00:15:00.980
Okay, but the first is sort
of a human observation.

00:15:00.980 --> 00:15:03.300
This is not anything you don't know

00:15:03.300 --> 00:15:05.620
but somehow I feel like for what we do,

00:15:05.620 --> 00:15:06.720
it's good to remember,

00:15:06.720 --> 00:15:08.260
which is that people crave community.

00:15:08.260 --> 00:15:10.872
Now, this photo was not taken last night.

00:15:10.872 --> 00:15:13.000
(audience laughing)

00:15:13.000 --> 00:15:13.833
Okay.

00:15:16.460 --> 00:15:17.722
I was up late.

00:15:17.722 --> 00:15:19.830
(audience laughing)

00:15:19.830 --> 00:15:23.573
I think I'm not appearing
grumpy, is that right?

00:15:23.573 --> 00:15:24.710
(audience laughing)

00:15:24.710 --> 00:15:26.810
But what I want to point
out about this photo is,

00:15:26.810 --> 00:15:28.540
so the first photo I started with

00:15:28.540 --> 00:15:32.420
was of a film processor
that was, you know,

00:15:32.420 --> 00:15:33.587
probably in the 80s or 70s

00:15:33.587 --> 00:15:35.470
and it has that, kind of almost like,

00:15:35.470 --> 00:15:39.020
cigarette smoke encased look to it, right?

00:15:39.020 --> 00:15:40.763
So let's date it as '83.

00:15:41.756 --> 00:15:44.230
You'd have to actually look at this photo

00:15:44.230 --> 00:15:45.990
quite carefully to date it.

00:15:45.990 --> 00:15:47.730
Okay, and it's probably
from within the last year,

00:15:47.730 --> 00:15:48.770
and I think the woman on the right,

00:15:48.770 --> 00:15:51.140
she's got these new-fangled beer bottles,

00:15:51.140 --> 00:15:53.600
and so you could date this if you had to.

00:15:53.600 --> 00:15:55.970
But the point is, from a
distance, if you just look at it.

00:15:55.970 --> 00:15:57.010
This kind of a scene,

00:15:57.010 --> 00:15:59.270
this kind of humans getting
crazy around a community,

00:15:59.270 --> 00:16:02.390
this would easily have been a
picture from the 80s, right?

00:16:02.390 --> 00:16:05.700
So I'm just saying, technology,
and you guys lead this,

00:16:05.700 --> 00:16:07.510
changes all the time.

00:16:07.510 --> 00:16:08.343
All the time.

00:16:08.343 --> 00:16:10.480
But the people we're
engaging with to use it,

00:16:10.480 --> 00:16:11.980
they don't change all that much.

00:16:11.980 --> 00:16:12.950
And, I don't know, I sort of feel like

00:16:12.950 --> 00:16:14.980
that's worth keeping in mind.

00:16:14.980 --> 00:16:15.920
All right.

00:16:15.920 --> 00:16:18.280
A second observation is that

00:16:18.280 --> 00:16:20.190
we should obsess over our audiences.

00:16:20.190 --> 00:16:22.383
So I try really hard,

00:16:24.040 --> 00:16:26.840
especially when talking with faculty,

00:16:26.840 --> 00:16:31.530
to go out of my way never,
ever, ever to appear,

00:16:31.530 --> 00:16:33.440
and this is like the sort of terrible word

00:16:33.440 --> 00:16:34.620
that can be used, corporate.

00:16:34.620 --> 00:16:35.746
Right?

00:16:35.746 --> 00:16:38.130
And I don't even say that like,

00:16:38.130 --> 00:16:39.580
they're so silly for having that fear.

00:16:39.580 --> 00:16:41.830
Like it's a fear that

00:16:41.830 --> 00:16:43.830
if you over-professionalize
communications,

00:16:43.830 --> 00:16:47.880
they will become disingenuous,
it'll become gloss,

00:16:47.880 --> 00:16:50.040
it'd just become kind
of lame and not genuine.

00:16:50.040 --> 00:16:51.610
And I totally hear that fear

00:16:51.610 --> 00:16:55.593
and I'm actually right there
with people who have that fear.

00:16:56.788 --> 00:16:59.050
But what I wanna say is that

00:16:59.050 --> 00:17:02.760
after doing my job for
like five years at MIT,

00:17:02.760 --> 00:17:03.593
and I got the hang of,

00:17:03.593 --> 00:17:05.796
I finally started to get the hang of it,

00:17:05.796 --> 00:17:08.880
I was learning less and less from my peers

00:17:08.880 --> 00:17:09.713
at other universities.

00:17:09.713 --> 00:17:11.213
I was still learning, and
definitely the connections

00:17:11.213 --> 00:17:13.400
that I was making with
them were super important

00:17:13.400 --> 00:17:14.979
and it was great to be able to call people

00:17:14.979 --> 00:17:17.010
whenever a crisis brewed

00:17:17.010 --> 00:17:18.790
and I knew that someone had
dealt with a similar issue,

00:17:18.790 --> 00:17:21.270
all that was good stuff,
but I wasn't really, like,

00:17:21.270 --> 00:17:23.620
furiously writing notes anymore
about how to do what I do.

00:17:23.620 --> 00:17:25.640
So I started to go to
conferences with people

00:17:25.640 --> 00:17:29.720
who do what we in communications
do in the private sector.

00:17:29.720 --> 00:17:31.810
So PR conferences where you're listening

00:17:31.810 --> 00:17:33.730
to the head of marketing
for, like, Coca Cola

00:17:33.730 --> 00:17:36.790
or Harley Davidson
motorcycles or whatever.

00:17:36.790 --> 00:17:40.433
And here's what I heard, I
still remember it obviously.

00:17:41.280 --> 00:17:43.350
I was at an economist
conference in New York

00:17:43.350 --> 00:17:45.420
like five years ago, and
there's a woman up on stage

00:17:45.420 --> 00:17:49.060
who is head of, I think
like Hilton brands,

00:17:49.060 --> 00:17:50.650
which was like, turns out to be like 14

00:17:50.650 --> 00:17:53.793
of the biggest and fanciest
hotel chains on the planet.

00:17:54.970 --> 00:17:59.450
And she said, you need to be
obsessed by your customers,

00:17:59.450 --> 00:18:00.730
just completely, and again,

00:18:00.730 --> 00:18:02.040
most of the people she's speaking with

00:18:02.040 --> 00:18:04.650
are people in the private sector, right?

00:18:04.650 --> 00:18:06.870
Just be obsessed with your customers.

00:18:06.870 --> 00:18:08.970
And I thought, you know, yeah.

00:18:08.970 --> 00:18:12.030
Yeah, that's the lesson to
take from this conference.

00:18:12.030 --> 00:18:12.960
Right?

00:18:12.960 --> 00:18:15.413
And so the way I think about it is,

00:18:17.050 --> 00:18:20.100
we would do ourselves a giant favor if,

00:18:20.100 --> 00:18:22.140
and it's not like we're not
doing this at all, right,

00:18:22.140 --> 00:18:25.100
but if we get really organized,
which we will in OPAC

00:18:25.100 --> 00:18:26.980
around measuring two types of things,

00:18:26.980 --> 00:18:29.760
we'll be really happy we did.

00:18:29.760 --> 00:18:31.820
So if we start by
identifying our audiences

00:18:31.820 --> 00:18:33.900
and do those concentric circles, right,

00:18:33.900 --> 00:18:36.367
from the campus to the alumni

00:18:36.367 --> 00:18:38.730
to the educated United States public

00:18:38.730 --> 00:18:40.500
to the educated global public.

00:18:40.500 --> 00:18:44.780
And then, say, local, state,
and federal governments.

00:18:44.780 --> 00:18:47.381
Branching to legislative and executive.

00:18:47.381 --> 00:18:51.990
You know, you can say
for all those audiences,

00:18:51.990 --> 00:18:54.860
I wanna know some basic things about,

00:18:54.860 --> 00:18:56.890
and this is what this
slide's meant to depict,

00:18:56.890 --> 00:18:58.800
people's sentiment, right?

00:18:58.800 --> 00:18:59.910
People's sentiment.

00:18:59.910 --> 00:19:04.910
So let's just say that he's
a grad student here, right?

00:19:05.040 --> 00:19:10.040
We want to know sentiment,
not just about Yale,

00:19:10.215 --> 00:19:11.915
but about the communications experiences

00:19:11.915 --> 00:19:13.930
that we are giving him.

00:19:13.930 --> 00:19:16.400
He's very happy, right?

00:19:16.400 --> 00:19:17.400
But this is what we wanna know,

00:19:17.400 --> 00:19:20.250
but if he were in the red
zone, we'd wanna know that, too

00:19:20.250 --> 00:19:22.020
and we'd wanna be able to act on that.

00:19:22.020 --> 00:19:23.310
And there are folks in this room

00:19:23.310 --> 00:19:27.220
who can run circles around
me in how to do that.

00:19:27.220 --> 00:19:30.187
And I invite you up here to
run circles around me on this.

00:19:30.187 --> 00:19:32.199
And then the second thing is,

00:19:32.199 --> 00:19:35.260
so this is, if this is sentiment,
like how do people feel

00:19:35.260 --> 00:19:39.650
both about Yale and about our
communications experiences,

00:19:39.650 --> 00:19:42.973
this is meant to depict behavior, right?

00:19:42.973 --> 00:19:46.300
We need to know something
about the digital behavior

00:19:46.300 --> 00:19:48.300
of the people we're engaging with.

00:19:48.300 --> 00:19:50.350
You know what I'm talking
about, the really basic stuff,

00:19:50.350 --> 00:19:55.350
like where are they going
on our overall websites.

00:19:56.030 --> 00:19:58.040
When we send them an email,
what's the open rate?

00:19:58.040 --> 00:19:59.420
When we send them an
email with lots of links,

00:19:59.420 --> 00:20:00.750
what are they linking to?

00:20:00.750 --> 00:20:04.310
How many seconds into a
video are they watching?

00:20:04.310 --> 00:20:07.170
What kinds of videos seem to
get them deep into the count?

00:20:07.170 --> 00:20:09.730
Which ones do they seem to stop watching?

00:20:09.730 --> 00:20:11.250
This is just data, right?

00:20:11.250 --> 00:20:15.560
And so, I don't know, if
you're gonna have an office of,

00:20:15.560 --> 00:20:18.790
say, 30 people working on communications

00:20:18.790 --> 00:20:20.120
on behalf of these audiences,

00:20:20.120 --> 00:20:22.730
it seems to me the investment
of a couple of people

00:20:22.730 --> 00:20:25.100
to try to be measuring stuff
can be hugely important.

00:20:25.100 --> 00:20:28.050
And I say this knowing that
there's lots of measurement

00:20:28.050 --> 00:20:33.050
going on across Yale, and
we will hope to the degree

00:20:33.390 --> 00:20:34.900
that people are willing to share it,

00:20:34.900 --> 00:20:36.480
to bottle some of that together

00:20:36.480 --> 00:20:40.411
and share that with
communications colleagues

00:20:40.411 --> 00:20:42.193
across the university.

00:20:44.090 --> 00:20:47.780
Another lesson I've learned
along the way is that

00:20:47.780 --> 00:20:52.020
you know, this is the famous
Forrester research depiction

00:20:52.020 --> 00:20:53.563
of customer engagement, right?

00:20:54.570 --> 00:20:56.280
The whole idea here is, and this is like,

00:20:56.280 --> 00:20:58.450
I feel like this is like
almost an accusation

00:20:58.450 --> 00:20:59.870
pointed at people like me.

00:20:59.870 --> 00:21:01.487
Like from friendly people
at Forrester, right?

00:21:01.487 --> 00:21:03.950
And the accusation is, you nitwits think

00:21:03.950 --> 00:21:05.780
that the way that you engage with people

00:21:05.780 --> 00:21:09.310
is to write breathless prose about,

00:21:09.310 --> 00:21:11.100
you know, about how great a place is.

00:21:11.100 --> 00:21:13.210
To which I would say,
yeah, that is my job.

00:21:13.210 --> 00:21:14.300
And they would say, yes, yes, yes,

00:21:14.300 --> 00:21:16.230
of course you need to do
that, of course you do.

00:21:16.230 --> 00:21:18.220
But what you need to
start with is utility.

00:21:18.220 --> 00:21:20.583
You need to give people what they want,

00:21:21.730 --> 00:21:24.240
and then they're more
likely to listen to you.

00:21:24.240 --> 00:21:26.250
And so, I actually found,

00:21:26.250 --> 00:21:29.500
this sounded good when
people explained it to me.

00:21:29.500 --> 00:21:31.560
And lo and behold,

00:21:31.560 --> 00:21:33.670
I had a specific instance at MIT

00:21:33.670 --> 00:21:35.470
where I found it to be true

00:21:35.470 --> 00:21:37.330
and to be hugely valuable to understand.

00:21:37.330 --> 00:21:40.060
So there was, within the
school of engineering,

00:21:40.060 --> 00:21:42.040
which is half of MIT,

00:21:42.040 --> 00:21:45.480
they actually did an
audit of what is on offer

00:21:45.480 --> 00:21:48.210
on all the departmental websites

00:21:48.210 --> 00:21:50.770
and an audit of what happens with people

00:21:50.770 --> 00:21:51.960
and what they're reading.

00:21:51.960 --> 00:21:53.270
And lo and behold,

00:21:53.270 --> 00:21:55.780
people didn't want the
inspirational stories.

00:21:55.780 --> 00:21:58.120
They wanted stuff about like
how do I get financial aid?

00:21:58.120 --> 00:21:59.670
And where are the offices?

00:21:59.670 --> 00:22:01.310
And what's the calendar, right?

00:22:01.310 --> 00:22:03.410
Utilitarian stuff.

00:22:03.410 --> 00:22:06.450
So that is very important.

00:22:06.450 --> 00:22:08.080
Okay, and the other lesson I've learned

00:22:08.080 --> 00:22:10.360
is that humans crave story.

00:22:10.360 --> 00:22:12.770
And this is, again, kind of right back

00:22:12.770 --> 00:22:16.920
to the people wanting to
be part of a community.

00:22:16.920 --> 00:22:17.811
Okay.

00:22:17.811 --> 00:22:22.811
So, I'll tell you why you're
seeing this image in a second.

00:22:22.990 --> 00:22:25.520
All right, so I was
talking to Tom Friedman,

00:22:25.520 --> 00:22:28.370
columnist at New York Times.

00:22:28.370 --> 00:22:31.940
The mover of markets, the
mover of public opinion, right?

00:22:31.940 --> 00:22:34.640
And I'd gotten to know Tom
a little bit over the years

00:22:35.750 --> 00:22:38.960
'cause he'd gotten very fond
of the President of MIT.

00:22:38.960 --> 00:22:41.430
And so I am with him and occasion is

00:22:41.430 --> 00:22:45.510
I just cannot wait to pitch
him a story for his column

00:22:45.510 --> 00:22:49.940
about MIT's giant ambitions
in online education.

00:22:49.940 --> 00:22:50.880
And I am loaded for bear

00:22:50.880 --> 00:22:53.450
because I've got one-on-one
time with Tom Friedman.

00:22:53.450 --> 00:22:54.560
How cool is that?

00:22:54.560 --> 00:22:57.560
So I've got, in my
pocket, tons of statistics

00:22:57.560 --> 00:23:00.440
that are super impressive
about the growth of this,

00:23:00.440 --> 00:23:02.800
the engagement and all that stuff.

00:23:02.800 --> 00:23:05.430
So Tom's a savvy guy, and so we sit down

00:23:05.430 --> 00:23:07.300
and I'm about to start talking

00:23:07.300 --> 00:23:09.380
and it's as if he has read my mind

00:23:09.380 --> 00:23:12.370
and knows that I'm going
to spit some numbers at him

00:23:12.370 --> 00:23:13.810
because what he said to me was,

00:23:13.810 --> 00:23:16.630
Nate, I'm definitely interested in this,

00:23:16.630 --> 00:23:18.370
but I just wanna say to you right now,

00:23:18.370 --> 00:23:22.140
I want to explain this to
my readers with stories,

00:23:22.140 --> 00:23:22.973
not numbers.

00:23:24.800 --> 00:23:27.550
And I thought, okay, and I said,

00:23:27.550 --> 00:23:30.300
ah, well let me tell you the story, Tom.

00:23:30.300 --> 00:23:32.010
So I told him the following story.

00:23:32.010 --> 00:23:36.360
So the first online course
that was offered at MIT

00:23:36.360 --> 00:23:40.650
was 6002X, which was
Circuits and Electronics.

00:23:40.650 --> 00:23:43.130
To do this course, you had to have had

00:23:43.130 --> 00:23:45.500
Differential Equations, okay?

00:23:45.500 --> 00:23:47.650
And we kind of purposely
put something out there

00:23:47.650 --> 00:23:50.130
that was, like, not L-I-T-E, right?

00:23:50.130 --> 00:23:53.260
This was gonna be like, you
want online education from MIT?

00:23:53.260 --> 00:23:56.270
Like, here you go, right?

00:23:56.270 --> 00:23:57.780
And it was electric.

00:23:57.780 --> 00:24:00.990
People, as we suspected, there
were so many more people,

00:24:00.990 --> 00:24:02.640
whether it's Yale or MIT around the world

00:24:02.640 --> 00:24:03.882
who can do the kind of work

00:24:03.882 --> 00:24:05.400
that we will put in front of them

00:24:05.400 --> 00:24:07.910
than can get here to campus.

00:24:07.910 --> 00:24:10.460
It was just sensational, the
number of people doing this.

00:24:10.460 --> 00:24:12.590
But anyway, as part of the
rudimentary first draft

00:24:12.590 --> 00:24:14.920
of this course, there was kind of

00:24:16.548 --> 00:24:18.130
the equivalent of a chat room, right,

00:24:18.130 --> 00:24:19.430
or just a message board.

00:24:19.430 --> 00:24:22.077
And somebody posted in the
message board from Cairo,

00:24:22.077 --> 00:24:25.297
"This course is too hard. I can't do it."

00:24:26.850 --> 00:24:29.670
Another person in Cairo saw that message,

00:24:29.670 --> 00:24:32.267
and said, "No, no, no, no.
You can totally do this.

00:24:32.267 --> 00:24:36.880
"Meet me and my friend at this
tea shop, we'll talk it out."

00:24:36.880 --> 00:24:38.400
So they met at a tea shop in Cairo

00:24:38.400 --> 00:24:40.600
and that person's back
in the course, right?

00:24:41.440 --> 00:24:43.330
That made it into Tom Friedman's column,

00:24:43.330 --> 00:24:44.400
so story matters.

00:24:44.400 --> 00:24:46.290
But the second reason I raise that course

00:24:46.290 --> 00:24:50.810
is that here you have an
example of a digital technology

00:24:50.810 --> 00:24:53.010
leading to human beings
gathered around something

00:24:53.010 --> 00:24:54.380
that was important to them.

00:24:54.380 --> 00:24:55.712
Okay.

00:24:55.712 --> 00:24:57.312
And I just like that photo, too.

00:24:59.500 --> 00:25:01.170
I think this my last sort of note,

00:25:01.170 --> 00:25:03.140
and I'm gonna wrap up in a second.

00:25:03.140 --> 00:25:03.973
Networks matter.

00:25:03.973 --> 00:25:04.930
Again, for folks in this room,

00:25:04.930 --> 00:25:06.590
this is kind of like, okay,

00:25:06.590 --> 00:25:08.970
you've discovered the internet, Nate,

00:25:08.970 --> 00:25:10.883
that's good, it's a good start.

00:25:13.490 --> 00:25:15.590
But here's the deal, so networks matter,

00:25:15.590 --> 00:25:19.150
I think they should matter if
people who do communications.

00:25:19.150 --> 00:25:22.100
And so I'm gonna show you,
this was sketched out.

00:25:22.100 --> 00:25:22.933
I was at a conference

00:25:22.933 --> 00:25:25.800
and the founders of
Buzzfeed and I were talking,

00:25:25.800 --> 00:25:28.020
and they actually did pull out a napkin

00:25:28.020 --> 00:25:29.290
and explained the following to me.

00:25:29.290 --> 00:25:31.990
This was them explaining how
they thought about the world.

00:25:31.990 --> 00:25:34.600
So on the left, what you
see is their depiction

00:25:34.600 --> 00:25:39.600
of how a traditional publisher
feels about her reader, okay?

00:25:39.600 --> 00:25:42.017
She is thinking all about her
relationship with this reader

00:25:42.017 --> 00:25:43.757
and this reader and this
reader and this reader.

00:25:43.757 --> 00:25:46.870
And she, that is her
obsession, which is great.

00:25:46.870 --> 00:25:48.863
How am I going to please that reader?

00:25:49.780 --> 00:25:50.910
I want to understand him.

00:25:50.910 --> 00:25:53.300
I want to give him what he wants.

00:25:53.300 --> 00:25:54.900
I want to send him solicitations

00:25:54.900 --> 00:25:57.510
that will lead to subscriptions.

00:25:57.510 --> 00:25:59.589
And I want to be able
to say to advertisers,

00:25:59.589 --> 00:26:02.890
this guy is the kind of person
you want to get in front of.

00:26:02.890 --> 00:26:04.930
That is what a traditional publisher does.

00:26:04.930 --> 00:26:07.360
Buzzfeed said, yeah sure, we'll do that.

00:26:07.360 --> 00:26:09.250
So that's the first ring
of what you see up here.

00:26:09.250 --> 00:26:10.900
We'll definitely do that.

00:26:10.900 --> 00:26:13.550
But what we're really
interested in is their friends.

00:26:14.480 --> 00:26:16.780
And those people's
relationships with each other.

00:26:16.780 --> 00:26:21.780
And this, of course, is the scales.

00:26:22.730 --> 00:26:23.950
That's the whole point, right,

00:26:23.950 --> 00:26:25.830
is that you just keep drawing out

00:26:25.830 --> 00:26:28.640
the fish bone of people's
networks and then pretty soon,

00:26:28.640 --> 00:26:31.760
those relationships are so much
more valuable and important

00:26:31.760 --> 00:26:35.850
than your central
relationship with people.

00:26:35.850 --> 00:26:36.770
So why does this matter?

00:26:36.770 --> 00:26:39.210
It matters because if
we're gonna move sentiment

00:26:39.210 --> 00:26:41.960
and we're gonna move
behavior, we would do well,

00:26:41.960 --> 00:26:43.490
and again, folks in this
room understand this

00:26:43.490 --> 00:26:44.323
far better than I do.

00:26:44.323 --> 00:26:47.010
I just know it exists as a
thing to pay attention to.

00:26:47.010 --> 00:26:49.413
I don't know much about
what to do about it.

00:26:50.277 --> 00:26:52.690
But networks have great power for us

00:26:52.690 --> 00:26:56.340
to move sentiment and move behavior.

00:26:56.340 --> 00:26:57.226
By behavior, by the way,

00:26:57.226 --> 00:26:59.340
it sounds kind of almost like clinical.

00:26:59.340 --> 00:27:03.080
I'm talking about things
like giving to Yale

00:27:03.080 --> 00:27:06.616
or applying to Yale, accepting
an offer of admission

00:27:06.616 --> 00:27:09.330
to Yale, applying for a job at Yale.

00:27:09.330 --> 00:27:12.973
These are all behaviors that
we would seek to influence.

00:27:15.470 --> 00:27:18.920
So this is, this will be my last slide,

00:27:18.920 --> 00:27:23.030
and by the way, in the slide that follows,

00:27:23.030 --> 00:27:26.210
you can sort of loosely see

00:27:26.210 --> 00:27:29.320
all of you as part of this equation.

00:27:29.320 --> 00:27:32.370
I just chose ITS because
I'm thinking hard about ITS.

00:27:32.370 --> 00:27:34.943
But this is what I want, right?

00:27:36.370 --> 00:27:38.770
Like way before I came here to Yale,

00:27:38.770 --> 00:27:41.670
I said to anyone who would listen,

00:27:41.670 --> 00:27:45.850
the biggest pieces of
progress in communication

00:27:45.850 --> 00:27:50.430
at universities are gonna
happen when leaders in,

00:27:50.430 --> 00:27:51.480
so I'm looking at this room,

00:27:51.480 --> 00:27:52.620
leaders in communications,

00:27:52.620 --> 00:27:55.200
leaders in people who
do technology, right,

00:27:55.200 --> 00:27:56.130
get together and say,

00:27:56.130 --> 00:27:59.540
how are we engaging with our audiences?

00:27:59.540 --> 00:28:02.430
How's it working and can we work together?

00:28:02.430 --> 00:28:07.430
And so, you know, when I met
John and folks on his team,

00:28:07.740 --> 00:28:08.960
I just got this jolt.

00:28:08.960 --> 00:28:10.800
I was like this is the real thing.

00:28:10.800 --> 00:28:12.080
We can do this here.

00:28:12.080 --> 00:28:14.260
And I know it's already happening.

00:28:14.260 --> 00:28:18.982
We have that, we have deep
expertise in OPAC digitally

00:28:18.982 --> 00:28:21.420
and we have, certainly, expertise

00:28:21.420 --> 00:28:22.960
in straight up communications.

00:28:22.960 --> 00:28:24.330
But there's so much more, I think,

00:28:24.330 --> 00:28:26.970
that we can all do together.

00:28:26.970 --> 00:28:30.480
So, you know, I was told that
the kind of theme here is,

00:28:30.480 --> 00:28:32.488
in one way or another, a
strengthening this place

00:28:32.488 --> 00:28:34.823
through strengthening connections.

00:28:35.700 --> 00:28:38.322
I sort of leave you with the idea that

00:28:38.322 --> 00:28:41.210
to whatever degree we can
strengthen connections

00:28:41.210 --> 00:28:42.950
between those who think of themselves

00:28:42.950 --> 00:28:44.650
as primarily communications

00:28:44.650 --> 00:28:46.680
and those who think of
themselves primarily as IT

00:28:46.680 --> 00:28:49.800
or anyone in between as
most of the hands here are,

00:28:49.800 --> 00:28:54.240
we will do this place, and
the pursuit of its mission

00:28:54.240 --> 00:28:56.763
a dramatic amount of good.

00:28:56.763 --> 00:28:58.337
Anyway, thank you very much.

00:28:58.337 --> 00:29:01.504
(audience applauding)

00:29:10.380 --> 00:29:12.380
(music)

